{"fields":{"advisory":{"aliases":[],"categories":["memory-corruption"],"collection":"crates","cvss":null,"date":"2026-05-12","description":"`LruCache::pop()` in `lru` was not panic-safe. If the `Drop` implementation of a stored key panics during `pop()`, `self.detach()` is never called, leaving dangling pointers in the internal doubly-linked list.\r\n\r\nA subsequent cache operation that triggers eviction can then dereference these dangling pointers:\r\n- The node is freed from the map, but remains linked in the LRU list due to the skipped `detach()` call\r\n- When a new insertion causes eviction, the LRU traversal encounters the dangling pointer\r\n- This results in a write to already-freed memory during the eviction process\r\n\r\n## Impact\r\n\r\n- **CWE-416 (Use-After-Free):** memory corruption when subsequent cache operations access freed node pointers in the linked list\r\n- **CWE-415 (Double Free):** potential heap corruption when the same memory is freed multiple times\r\n\r\nBoth types of undefined behavior can be invoked in safe Rust, but only if unwinding panics are enabled and `std::panic::catch_unwind` is used with key types that have potentially-panicking `Drop` implementations.\r\n\r\n## Fix\r\n\r\nFixed in `lru` 0.18.2 by detaching the node from the linked list before freeing it and dropping the key ([lru-rs#238](https://github.com/jeromefroe/lru-rs/pull/238)).","expect-deleted":false,"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0253","informational":"unsound","keywords":["panic-safety","memory-safety","use-after-free","double-free"],"license":"CC0-1.0","package":"lru","references":[],"related":[],"source":null,"title":"Potential use-after-free due to lack of panic safety in `LruCache::pop()`","url":"https://github.com/jeromefroe/lru-rs/pull/238","withdrawn":null},"code":"unsound","graphs":[{"Krate":{"name":"lru","version":"0.17.0"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"chitchat","version":"0.11.1"}}]}],"labels":[{"column":1,"line":34,"message":"unsound advisory detected","span":"lru 0.17.0 registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"message":"Potential use-after-free due to lack of panic safety in `LruCache::pop()`","notes":["ID: RUSTSEC-2026-0253","Advisory: https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0253","`LruCache::pop()` in `lru` was not panic-safe. If the `Drop` implementation of a stored key panics during `pop()`, `self.detach()` is never called, leaving dangling pointers in the internal doubly-linked list.\r\n\r\nA subsequent cache operation that triggers eviction can then dereference these dangling pointers:\r\n- The node is freed from the map, but remains linked in the LRU list due to the skipped `detach()` call\r\n- When a new insertion causes eviction, the LRU traversal encounters the dangling pointer\r\n- This results in a write to already-freed memory during the eviction process\r\n\r\n## Impact\r\n\r\n- **CWE-416 (Use-After-Free):** memory corruption when subsequent cache operations access freed node pointers in the linked list\r\n- **CWE-415 (Double Free):** potential heap corruption when the same memory is freed multiple times\r\n\r\nBoth types of undefined behavior can be invoked in safe Rust, but only if unwinding panics are enabled and `std::panic::catch_unwind` is used with key types that have potentially-panicking `Drop` implementations.\r\n\r\n## Fix\r\n\r\nFixed in `lru` 0.18.2 by detaching the node from the linked list before freeing it and dropping the key ([lru-rs#238](https://github.com/jeromefroe/lru-rs/pull/238)).","Announcement: https://github.com/jeromefroe/lru-rs/pull/238","Solution: Upgrade to >=0.18.2 (try `cargo update -p lru`)"],"severity":"error"},"type":"diagnostic"}
{"fields":{"advisory":{"aliases":[],"categories":["memory-exposure"],"collection":"crates","cvss":null,"date":"2026-10-16","description":"The `with_context` constructors of the stream encoders and decoders used the\r\nborrowed context as it was. When a stream was abandoned on a context, for\r\nexample an encoder dropped before `finish()` after an error, whatever it\r\nleft there was carried into the next stream: the input buffered by the\r\nprevious encoder (up to 128 KiB, or all of it with `zstdmt`) became the\r\nstart of the next frame, and a decoder returned undelivered output of the\r\nprevious stream.\r\n\r\nAn application reusing one context for data that must stay separate, such\r\nas different users' requests, could disclose one user's plaintext to\r\nanother. Whoever supplies the data can cause the abandonment with invalid\r\ninput.\r\n\r\nThe flaw was corrected by resetting the context's session in\r\n`with_context`.","expect-deleted":false,"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0391","informational":null,"keywords":["information-leak","context-reuse"],"license":"CC0-1.0","package":"zstd","references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"related":[],"source":null,"title":"Data from an abandoned stream leaks into the next stream on a reused context","url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/74ace2dcdca62a2ce0f034895d6079458311e003","withdrawn":null},"code":"vulnerability","graphs":[{"Krate":{"name":"zstd","version":"0.13.3"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"chitchat","version":"0.11.1"}}]}],"labels":[{"column":1,"line":88,"message":"security vulnerability detected","span":"zstd 0.13.3 registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"message":"Data from an abandoned stream leaks into the next stream on a reused context","notes":["ID: RUSTSEC-2026-0391","Advisory: https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0391","The `with_context` constructors of the stream encoders and decoders used the\r\nborrowed context as it was. When a stream was abandoned on a context, for\r\nexample an encoder dropped before `finish()` after an error, whatever it\r\nleft there was carried into the next stream: the input buffered by the\r\nprevious encoder (up to 128 KiB, or all of it with `zstdmt`) became the\r\nstart of the next frame, and a decoder returned undelivered output of the\r\nprevious stream.\r\n\r\nAn application reusing one context for data that must stay separate, such\r\nas different users' requests, could disclose one user's plaintext to\r\nanother. Whoever supplies the data can cause the abandonment with invalid\r\ninput.\r\n\r\nThe flaw was corrected by resetting the context's session in\r\n`with_context`.","Announcement: https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/74ace2dcdca62a2ce0f034895d6079458311e003","Solution: Upgrade to >=0.14.3 (try `cargo update -p zstd`)"],"severity":"error"},"type":"diagnostic"}
{"fields":{"advisory":{"aliases":[],"categories":["memory-exposure","memory-corruption"],"collection":"crates","cvss":null,"date":"2026-10-16","description":"`ZSTD_seekable_decompress` shortens the requested length to the end of the\r\ndata without checking that the offset is before it. For an offset past the\r\nend, it returns a wrapped-around length without writing anything, and\r\n`Seekable::decompress` passed that length to `Vec::set_len`.\r\n\r\nWith the `seekable` feature, whoever supplies the archive, or chooses the\r\noffset, can leave the caller's `Vec<u8>` with a length far beyond its\r\ncapacity. Reading it then discloses neighbouring heap memory, and appending\r\nto it corrupts memory. Offsets near `u64::MAX` can also hang the call.\r\n\r\nThe flaw was corrected by clamping requests to the end of the data, and by\r\nchecking every count reported by the C library against the buffer capacity.","expect-deleted":false,"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0398","informational":null,"keywords":["seekable","out-of-bounds"],"license":"CC0-1.0","package":"zstd-safe","references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"related":[],"source":null,"title":"`Seekable::decompress` sets a `Vec` length beyond its capacity for offsets past the end","url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/80506be743fae2ed0c2fe8b21c374c6a2eab0be7","withdrawn":null},"code":"vulnerability","graphs":[{"Krate":{"name":"zstd-safe","version":"7.2.4"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"zstd","version":"0.13.3"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"chitchat","version":"0.11.1"}}]}]}],"labels":[{"column":1,"line":89,"message":"security vulnerability detected","span":"zstd-safe 7.2.4 registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"message":"`Seekable::decompress` sets a `Vec` length beyond its capacity for offsets past the end","notes":["ID: RUSTSEC-2026-0398","Advisory: https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0398","`ZSTD_seekable_decompress` shortens the requested length to the end of the\r\ndata without checking that the offset is before it. For an offset past the\r\nend, it returns a wrapped-around length without writing anything, and\r\n`Seekable::decompress` passed that length to `Vec::set_len`.\r\n\r\nWith the `seekable` feature, whoever supplies the archive, or chooses the\r\noffset, can leave the caller's `Vec<u8>` with a length far beyond its\r\ncapacity. Reading it then discloses neighbouring heap memory, and appending\r\nto it corrupts memory. Offsets near `u64::MAX` can also hang the call.\r\n\r\nThe flaw was corrected by clamping requests to the end of the data, and by\r\nchecking every count reported by the C library against the buffer capacity.","Announcement: https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/80506be743fae2ed0c2fe8b21c374c6a2eab0be7","Solution: Upgrade to >=8.2.0 (try `cargo update -p zstd-safe`)"],"severity":"error"},"type":"diagnostic"}
{"fields":{"advisory":{"aliases":[],"categories":["memory-exposure"],"collection":"crates","cvss":null,"date":"2026-10-16","description":"When decompression fails inside a legacy (zstd v0.4 to v0.7) frame, the\r\nbundled zstd library reports the output buffer as fully written although\r\nnothing was written. `DCtx::decompress_stream` committed that position even\r\non error, and for a `Vec<u8>` destination this extended the vector's length\r\nover uninitialized memory.\r\n\r\nLegacy support is enabled by default. A 5- or 6-byte corrupt legacy frame is\r\nenough: through `zstd::stream::write::Decoder`, up to 32 KiB of stale heap\r\nmemory is then passed to the wrapped writer on the next `write()` or\r\n`flush()`, or when an `auto_flush()`/`on_flush()` decoder is dropped. Whether\r\nthis reaches an attacker depends on where the application sends the\r\ndecompressed output. `zstd::stream::read::Decoder`, `zstd::decode_all` and\r\nthe bulk API are not affected.\r\n\r\nBefore zstd-safe 4.0.0, output buffers could only be initialized slices.\r\nThe zstd crate's own writer had the same flaw from 0.4.22 to 0.7.x, which is\r\ncovered by a separate advisory.\r\n\r\nThe flaw was corrected by only committing the buffer positions of a\r\nsuccessful call.","expect-deleted":false,"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0403","informational":null,"keywords":["uninitialized-memory","decompression","legacy"],"license":"CC0-1.0","package":"zstd-safe","references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"related":[],"source":null,"title":"Uninitialized memory exposed when streaming decompression of a legacy frame fails","url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/006e1d1ad1eeaf6b5d4c28329e70217adabcec43","withdrawn":null},"code":"vulnerability","graphs":[{"Krate":{"name":"zstd-safe","version":"7.2.4"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"zstd","version":"0.13.3"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"chitchat","version":"0.11.1"}}]}]}],"labels":[{"column":1,"line":89,"message":"security vulnerability detected","span":"zstd-safe 7.2.4 registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"message":"Uninitialized memory exposed when streaming decompression of a legacy frame fails","notes":["ID: RUSTSEC-2026-0403","Advisory: https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0403","When decompression fails inside a legacy (zstd v0.4 to v0.7) frame, the\r\nbundled zstd library reports the output buffer as fully written although\r\nnothing was written. `DCtx::decompress_stream` committed that position even\r\non error, and for a `Vec<u8>` destination this extended the vector's length\r\nover uninitialized memory.\r\n\r\nLegacy support is enabled by default. A 5- or 6-byte corrupt legacy frame is\r\nenough: through `zstd::stream::write::Decoder`, up to 32 KiB of stale heap\r\nmemory is then passed to the wrapped writer on the next `write()` or\r\n`flush()`, or when an `auto_flush()`/`on_flush()` decoder is dropped. Whether\r\nthis reaches an attacker depends on where the application sends the\r\ndecompressed output. `zstd::stream::read::Decoder`, `zstd::decode_all` and\r\nthe bulk API are not affected.\r\n\r\nBefore zstd-safe 4.0.0, output buffers could only be initialized slices.\r\nThe zstd crate's own writer had the same flaw from 0.4.22 to 0.7.x, which is\r\ncovered by a separate advisory.\r\n\r\nThe flaw was corrected by only committing the buffer positions of a\r\nsuccessful call.","Announcement: https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/006e1d1ad1eeaf6b5d4c28329e70217adabcec43","Solution: Upgrade to >=8.2.0 (try `cargo update -p zstd-safe`)"],"severity":"error"},"type":"diagnostic"}
